{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001's distinguishing evidence (divergence-stratum advantage) fails to separate genuine reliability gain from mere checklist compliance that tracks the right answer. The target argues the benefit must concentrate where the checklist's indicated action diverges from the panel's default, treating divergence-stratum advantage as evidence of genuine information content. But if the checklist encodes a decision rule that happens to track the right answer without the panel understanding WHY, divergence cases are exactly where rote compliance produces the largest apparent improvement — the panel follows an alien rule it would not have used, precisely because the rule says so, not because it has acquired genuine reliability. The target's own overturn condition admits the divergence stratum is confounded by difficulty (all hard cases are divergence cases), but it does not resolve this confound: a compliance-driven panel could show its entire advantage in the divergence stratum simply because that is where following instructions matters most. Thus the 'divergence-stratum advantage' observable cannot distinguish genuine reliability gain from mere compliance that happens to track the right answer, which is exactly what the problem demands the evidence do.","counterexample":null,"target_alias":"SRC_001"},{"attack":false,"target_alias":"SRC_002"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_03's corrupted-checklist control is not reliably feasible 'within a single evaluation,' and the target's own counterconditions acknowledge this. The problem explicitly asks for evidence within a single evaluation. The control requires splitting the panel into sub-panels and silently giving one a corrupted checklist. For many tasks with objectively verifiable outcomes, the corruption (shuffled criteria, reversed thresholds) is either detectable by panel members (who may notice internally inconsistent or nonsensical rules), or the split destroys the panel-size/noise-reduction property that the original claim is about, making the sub-panel comparison inapt. The countercondition 'The control is not feasible within a single evaluation' concedes the entire discriminating test can fail to apply. Since the target stakes the ENTIRE ability to distinguish genuine from compliance-driven reliability on this one control ('only if the evaluation includes a corrupted-checklist or shuffled-criteria control'), the infeasibility cases are not edge cases — they collapse the proposal's coverage for the precise problem posed.","counterexample":null,"target_alias":"SRC_003"}]}